# LUCI commit cadence — keep the safety net intact

**Why this exists:** a month of uncommitted work once made every agent edit a gamble — no `git revert` possible, painful manual undos. A checkpoint fixed it once; this rule keeps it fixed. A clean `main` is what makes branches, deploys, and "try 3 variants" actually safe.

## Commit in small logical chunks — proactively

Don't wait to be asked. When a coherent block of work is done, commit it:
- A section / page / component built and verified
- A rule or doc added/updated
- A set of user-approved edits applied
- A deploy completed (commit the source that produced it)
- A bug fixed

One commit per logical unit, not one giant dump at the end of a session. If a change spans two unrelated things, it's two commits.

## Commit messages

Descriptive, present-tense, say *why* not just *what*. Match the repo's existing style (e.g. "System architecture diagram: standardize on black rack, consistent rack labels…"). For routine work, just commit. For large / ambiguous / structural changes, draft the message and surface it to the user first.

## Before risky or experimental work — commit first

Before branching, before a redesign attempt, before a "let me try a different direction," before a bulk find/replace — commit current work so `main` is a known-good baseline you can return to. This is what makes `luci-visual-options-workflow.mdc`'s 3-variants-on-branches safe: branches branch from a clean, committed `main`.

## Before ending a session

If there's uncommitted work, commit it. If it's genuinely incomplete/scratch, commit with a `wip:` prefix and a note so it's at least recoverable. Don't hand back a dirty working tree.

## Never commit

- Secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys) — they're gitignored; still double-check before `git add -A`.
- Scratch / debug output (`.texcheck/` is gitignored; keep it out).
- Half-broken states that would leave `main` unusable — finish the work, or `wip:`-prefix with a note.

## Branches for experiments, `main` for truth

`main` should always be deployable. Anything exploratory or "might be worse" goes on a branch (see `luci-visual-options-workflow.mdc`), merged only after the user picks the winner. Don't push broken experiments into `main`.

## When in doubt

Commit. The cost of an extra commit is near zero; the cost of losing uncommitted work to a bad agent edit is high. Bias toward committing.
